#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228886 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d9576e0-3be5-5d05-8cc8-c3ecde496b46" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Solutions of Homogeneous BVPs"}] :question/id #uuid "b40412bb-d2d7-519e-aad6-60f53f4a1fcd" :question/math-academy-id "e-21797" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\ny'' - 2y' - 3y = 0,  \\qquad y'(1) = y'(3) = 0,\\quad 1\\le x\\le3\n$$\n\nLet $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/assets/aa58b04d0ebdd18e4259fd3a1386332043f317f12b94c7b4ad468bef77584a0f.png)\n\nFind the fundamental solutions and use $A$ to determine if the given BVP has a unique or infinitely many solutions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\ny''(x)+P(x)y'(x)+Q(x)y=0,\\qquad a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_{1}y(a)+\\beta_{1}y'(a)=0,\\qquad\n\\alpha_{2}y(b)+\\beta_{2}y'(b)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and $\\alpha_i, \\beta_i$ are real constants such that $(\\alpha_i,\\beta_i)\\ne(0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$  \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/assets/ac7be07071df654f4d2fec18b6b93afb21fd13169e7890ea091b496d7f9b206c.png)\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y\\equiv0$ if and only if $\\det(A)\\ne0.$\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions.  So, setting $\\alpha_i=0,$ $\\beta_i=1,$ $a=1,$ and $b=3,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/assets/91f85685d5a17221398842923709c20b0e296e53fedb30ee6a4fdcb16d99e0d8.png)\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y=e^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-2m-3 & =0 \\\\ (m-3)(m+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=3$ and $m=-1,$ and so the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_{1}(x)=e^{3x}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;y_{2}(x)=e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny_1'(x) = 3e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2'(x) = -e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3(1)} & -{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{3(3)} & -{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3} & -{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{9} & -{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(3{e}^{3})(-{e}^{-3})-(-{e}^{-1})(3{e}^{9}) \\\\  & =-3+3{e}^{8} \\\\  & =3({e}^{8}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)\\ne0,$ we conclude that this BVP has the unique trivial solution $y\\equiv0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228888 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4607104f-d91b-50b8-a446-377eb37de5a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Homogeneous BVPs With Nontrivial Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "b6c06f26-9ff1-5e60-b0e3-5ee7482dff34" :question/math-academy-id "e-21799" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\ny'' = 0, \\qquad y(4) = 0, \\quad y(3) + y'(3) = 0,\\quad 3\\leq x \\leq 4\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nWe have the equation\n\n$$\ny''(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny' = A.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(4) = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n4A + B = 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad B = -4A.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny = Ax - 4A = A(x-4).\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(3) + y'(3) = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-A+A = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y = A\\cdot \\left(x-4\\right),$\nwhere $A$ is arbitrary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d9576e0-3be5-5d05-8cc8-c3ecde496b46" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Solutions of Homogeneous BVPs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0e6bc2b-7da1-5428-a625-a9092c510f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1 − e^{4})/(e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228426} {:answer/id #uuid "c1087e67-5046-51c5-9380-ce4f875af4a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{2} + e^{4})/(e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228427} {:answer/id #uuid "38e4ca5a-4e04-575c-b5af-ec9d43fa38c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228428} {:answer/id #uuid "52b0ba27-8a59-510f-b207-4a22e48b75ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos (1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228429} {:answer/id #uuid "761aea0c-1d32-59f3-95f5-a2640a4b4f53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 − e^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0e6bc2b-7da1-5428-a625-a9092c510f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1 − e^{4})/(e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228426} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f9ebf19-22b0-5923-b28b-a1c31951131b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228425} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce4981bf-def3-5b7b-b715-20fcecf0e522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228432} {:answer/id #uuid "322c202d-1949-5091-a1ab-2cf4b3b46ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228433} {:answer/id #uuid "56a13b61-2015-5809-9122-92f24c3a4fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56a13b61-2015-5809-9122-92f24c3a4fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228434} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e5b690f-d357-53b4-bc5c-ce9f9fd6d5f9" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228431} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3ef8ea1-dd48-59b6-98ca-0b4b4df69ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A) ≠ 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228436} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd473dd-10d8-5658-aaa1-12d5a8b9d7fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A) = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3ef8ea1-dd48-59b6-98ca-0b4b4df69ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A) ≠ 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228436} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bd62d9d-8d0d-5e25-9e73-725a5394529f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228435} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eb6bc51-a778-51ac-a016-3ffe06a42290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373197} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee0444d-1993-5b9e-8160-1b5a71a856b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373198} {:answer/id #uuid "b01cc755-bf8e-533d-9b06-6779accc1cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373199} {:answer/id #uuid "b4298bd8-b56a-5198-b598-9ff14d04590b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9eb6bc51-a778-51ac-a016-3ffe06a42290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373197} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9cc9012-ec22-5d99-bbbf-c417d8a79722" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cdee9cef-7184-5408-a29d-c97a87168d80" :question/math-academy-id "q-338525" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-3y=0,\\,y(0)=y(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, we have $det(A)=$  {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, this BVP {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Dirichlet boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=1,{\\beta }_{i}=0,a=0,$ and $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-2m-3 & =0 \\\\ (m-3)(m+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=3$ and $m=-1,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}\\text{ and }{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ {e}^{3} & {e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={e}^{-1}-{e}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-{e}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{4}}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has the unique solution }y≡0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4607104f-d91b-50b8-a446-377eb37de5a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Homogeneous BVPs With Nontrivial Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "199fe473-da76-5426-bfd9-5fc5eac3d897" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "199fe473-da76-5426-bfd9-5fc5eac3d897" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373201} :answer-field/id #uuid "57812598-2970-5fd9-93fd-7f337c6d99d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "382e2b54-213f-5d6d-8b77-7c5a76835556" :question/math-academy-id "q-338544" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0,\\,y(-2)=0,\\,y(2)-4{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,-2\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nWe have the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(-2)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-2A+B=0\\,⇒\\,B=2A.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=Ax+2A=A(x+2).\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(2)-4{y}^{′}(2)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n4A-4A=0\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot (x+2),$ where $A$ is arbitrary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4607104f-d91b-50b8-a446-377eb37de5a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Homogeneous BVPs With Nontrivial Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95a4e6b8-51a7-5b47-aff1-0b01772c7583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373204} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4311d2-2349-5f81-a1cc-7ed85395c410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95a4e6b8-51a7-5b47-aff1-0b01772c7583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373204} :answer-field/id #uuid "b869d149-4c40-554e-8214-fce0ef29583e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5406da4-e587-583d-979b-bb4ba17a2f3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-338599" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0,\\,y(-4)=0,\\,y(0)-4{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,-4\\le x\\le 0\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nWe have the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(-4)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-4A+B=0\\,⇒\\,B=4A.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=Ax+4A=A(x+4).\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(0)-4{y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n4A-4A=0\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot (x+4),$ where $A$ is arbitrary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4607104f-d91b-50b8-a446-377eb37de5a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Homogeneous BVPs With Nontrivial Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074ddc79-c44b-55e4-8cb5-be16e7cf49e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "074ddc79-c44b-55e4-8cb5-be16e7cf49e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373208} :answer-field/id #uuid "b798a131-4bda-5e02-a47b-66aad8b279e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "037aa2da-018b-5f7b-9bc8-1ee3e00b1d17" :question/math-academy-id "q-338645" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-16y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)-4y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}\\,(\\frac{1}{2})-4y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4.$ So, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4A{e}^{4x}-4B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(0)-4y(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4A-4B)-4(A+B) & =0 \\\\ -8B & =0 \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}\\,(\\frac{1}{2})-4y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n4A{e}^{2}-4A{e}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot {e}^{4x},$ where $A$ is arbitrary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d9576e0-3be5-5d05-8cc8-c3ecde496b46" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Solutions of Homogeneous BVPs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ded9ee41-3525-5e65-b042-bbd015dfed5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A)\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373211} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8e5b31-fc82-5632-8d85-4bfbfdddaf5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ded9ee41-3525-5e65-b042-bbd015dfed5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A)\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373211} :answer-field/id #uuid "de739df2-97fc-52ef-ae18-e90fff94d89a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373210} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6ad8cba-8023-535b-b91d-0cc19aa4d694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373214} {:answer/id #uuid "5f38ae83-68ec-5fce-9047-656a1f32b27c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373215} {:answer/id #uuid "e15a8aa0-6f21-5ae6-9afd-d73877b5140e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373216} {:answer/id #uuid "c18f739e-5cfe-52f2-9e32-e80cf8595a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f38ae83-68ec-5fce-9047-656a1f32b27c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373215} :answer-field/id #uuid "46606f3f-ec1a-5556-9198-29b214403fbe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373213} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c50bc5e1-9b07-5570-85ce-f6c35b22dee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373219} {:answer/id #uuid "0597eb6a-3c67-5d3f-8247-e031b44c7a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373220} {:answer/id #uuid "bded66fd-3a2c-5cdd-8a66-4132ff8d874d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373221} {:answer/id #uuid "bb437e6a-bb97-55c5-a08f-f99ee05e0592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373222} {:answer/id #uuid "75eacf40-724a-5ca1-8bce-96286f00642a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}+1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0597eb6a-3c67-5d3f-8247-e031b44c7a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373220} :answer-field/id #uuid "7305bc72-5483-59d9-b9ab-ed5490df93ab" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373218} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd666ab4-8745-5418-ac64-cb03a8791d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373225} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e8f728-51d4-582e-bab2-f17180b7ed20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373226} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ed7ac2-6ab5-508c-b60c-dacbd7b6f49d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd666ab4-8745-5418-ac64-cb03a8791d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373225} :answer-field/id #uuid "fec5e295-18c4-5434-80ab-8be83bcc0ab5" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2dbc0eba-24a7-50e0-964f-4c8949e5821f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338686" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-2y=0,\\,y(0)=y(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, we have $det(A)=$ {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, this BVP {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Dirichlet boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=1,{\\beta }_{i}=0,a=0,$ and $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-m-2 & =0 \\\\ (m-2)(m+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=2$ and $m=-1,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\text{ and }{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ {e}^{2} & {e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={e}^{-1}-{e}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-{e}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has the unique solution }y≡0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d9576e0-3be5-5d05-8cc8-c3ecde496b46" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Solutions of Homogeneous BVPs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97b5eb50-f677-5075-8a8d-901968baaac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A)\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373230} {:answer/id #uuid "93d6aefc-013e-5c57-906a-69b94b46c801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97b5eb50-f677-5075-8a8d-901968baaac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A)\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373230} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaac056c-4b06-59da-ac6b-5e1f0aa4f8d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373229} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec29606f-15cb-58f3-9b47-1d410b6857de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373233} {:answer/id #uuid "7f1535bb-caf0-5d91-a232-faeb724d1bd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373234} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8a7a33-068e-5fc3-9fd4-4eda48ee08c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373235} {:answer/id #uuid "6de64104-ecfc-5bc1-a3e8-79b9f5270e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e8a7a33-068e-5fc3-9fd4-4eda48ee08c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373235} :answer-field/id #uuid "adf544eb-0e10-538c-852f-b25534b57ba4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373232} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a9a9ee-da96-5cae-b8af-1f771718c706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373238} {:answer/id #uuid "a88713d3-253e-5967-b4b7-f406fa684e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373239} {:answer/id #uuid "80c511aa-cd59-544b-9984-6bdb49004599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373240} {:answer/id #uuid "27d4ba68-d5d3-516d-b339-629a183df411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6e(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373241} {:answer/id #uuid "e4984aa3-f26f-5e85-b382-6e109bf95906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a88713d3-253e-5967-b4b7-f406fa684e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373239} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd761eb0-bb58-51cb-9edf-c2c60d693584" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373237} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7833855b-efd1-5336-97fe-cc390f9420f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373244} {:answer/id #uuid "42952755-372d-5037-8951-f2eadb6477f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373245} {:answer/id #uuid "fe58d4ca-b2ff-5810-afbf-e03df157e6a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7833855b-efd1-5336-97fe-cc390f9420f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373244} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9094d38-a62d-55cd-aa7e-3d7101f7b3a8" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "852ae002-a1b4-555b-a8f4-0d108e87cf18" :question/math-academy-id "q-338980" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=y(1)-{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0)+{y}_{1}^{′}(0) & {y}_{2}(0)+{y}_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1)-{y}_{1}^{′}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)-{y}_{2}^{′}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, we have $det(A)=$  {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, this BVP {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Robin boundary conditions, given by\n\n$$\ny(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)-{y}^{′}(1)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, setting ${\\alpha }_{1}=1,$ ${\\beta }_{1}=1,$ ${\\alpha }_{2}=1,$ ${\\beta }_{2}=-1,$ and $a=0,$  $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ becomes\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0)+{y}_{1}^{′}(0) & {y}_{2}(0)+{y}_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1)-{y}_{1}^{′}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)-{y}_{2}^{′}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-3m+2 & =0 \\\\ (m-1)(m-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have ${m}_{1}=1$ and ${m}_{2}=2,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{′}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{0}+{e}^{0} & {e}^{3(0)}+2{e}^{2(0)} \\\\ {e}^{1}-{e}^{1} & {e}^{2(1)}-2{e}^{2(1)}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =2(-{e}^{2})-0(3) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has a unique solution }y≡0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d9576e0-3be5-5d05-8cc8-c3ecde496b46" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Solutions of Homogeneous BVPs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "604e5c8a-54be-5d79-a1e2-9bf13da6ff21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A)\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373249} {:answer/id #uuid "5d83d14e-80f2-50e8-a64e-30a6a2b20d18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "604e5c8a-54be-5d79-a1e2-9bf13da6ff21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A)\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373249} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5db0bdb-da71-52eb-bb19-e556b6d71abb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373248} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bbabb5b-9fc0-5386-a083-721c9971adc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373252} {:answer/id #uuid "99819e1c-2889-5e42-b9f9-2cce093af635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373253} {:answer/id #uuid "09ee4e7c-59aa-5efd-b703-b7e1ad22abb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$