xt} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373263} {:answer/id #uuid "59338b52-de61-5978-beb9-a722e900f206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d01b726-4aed-5d9c-8edc-6a5ea82038c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373263} :answer-field/id #uuid "191a4b37-d1fe-5649-a2be-e2335f7a6b16" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45c83d7b-ccbf-56c3-a6ca-9dacb006fa39" :question/math-academy-id "q-339097" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0,\\,y(0)=y(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi \n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(\\pi ) & {y}_{2}(\\pi )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, we have $det(A)=$  {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, this BVP {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Dirichlet boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=1,{\\beta }_{i}=0,a=0,$ and $b=\\pi ,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(\\pi ) & {y}_{2}(\\pi )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we solve the auxiliary equation\n\n$$\n{m}^{2}+1=0,\n$$\n\nwhich has roots $\\pm i.$ Thus, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{ and }{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =0(-1)-1(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)=0$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has infinitely many solutions.}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d9576e0-3be5-5d05-8cc8-c3ecde496b46" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Solutions of Homogeneous BVPs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b22af76e-2702-5ef9-b207-36e0c4c93cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A)\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373267} {:answer/id #uuid "948bc0f8-f3af-5350-80bc-989074863ffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b22af76e-2702-5ef9-b207-36e0c4c93cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.typ