r/id #uuid "5077653c-2f30-5b4e-aed7-1583fe75a7a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4({e}^{2}+{e}^{-2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373278} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc5871d-2fd3-5f34-a2c6-d8441ea07420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dc5871d-2fd3-5f34-a2c6-d8441ea07420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373279} :answer-field/id #uuid "0900a6ea-c0ea-522a-bb09-8b521463be29" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373274} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb3e8fd3-8f3b-591f-9b29-4d40c7164892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373281} {:answer/id #uuid "468ab820-f7f2-58a1-983d-c283f1d4decf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373282} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba4ff4e-c0af-5574-bcf0-e8fca7773130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "468ab820-f7f2-58a1-983d-c283f1d4decf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373282} :answer-field/id #uuid "c42f11fc-ae5d-5551-896f-cdeca7e9eb82" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "355bfeb1-d0b8-53cd-b763-3f92856140f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-339176" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4y=0,\\,2y(0)-{y}^{′}(0)=2y(1)-{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}(0)-{y}_{1}^{′}(0) & 2{y}_{2}(0)-{y}_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ 2{y}_{1}(1)-{y}_{1}^{′}(1) & 2{y}_{2}(1)-{y}_{2}^{′}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, we have $det(A)=$ {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, this BVP {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have mixed (Robin) boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=2,$ ${\\beta }_{i}=-1,$ $a=0,$ and $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}(0)-{y}_{1}^{′}(0) & 2{y}_{2}(0)-{y}_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ 2{y}_{1}(1)-{y}_{1}^{′}(1) & 2{y}_{2}(1)-{y}_{2}^{′}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (m-2)(m+2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=2$ and $m=-2,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\text{ and }{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}^{′}(x)=-2{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =0(4{e}^{-2})-0(4) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)=0$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has infinitely many solutions }.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4607104f-d91b-50b8-a446-377eb37de5a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Homogeneous BVPs With Nontrivial Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8f4b4d6-ec87-5e63-866d-8a598bf11a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8f4b4d6-ec87-5e63-866d-8a598bf11a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373286} :answer-field/id #uuid "219f513c-861f-5db4-9983-0e9899251310" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "64adb40a-93af-5684-81b2-e7031fa0c08c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339297" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)-y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)-y(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 1.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A{e}^{x}-B{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(0)-y(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-B-(A+B) & =0 \\\\ -2B & =0 \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(1)-y(1)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nA{e}^{1}-A{e}^{1}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is\n\n$$\ny=A\\cdot {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is arbitrary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4607104f-d91b-50b8-a446-377eb37de5a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Homogeneous BVPs With Nontrivial Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "625b866a-0d0f-5e3c-a699-0967fcf850dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373289} {:answer/id #uuid "a83222f6-580c-5d99-8658-fc12dde43786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "625b866a-0d0f-5e3c-a699-0967fcf850dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373289} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec8b1e5d-5284-578f-95d4-112672c62c00" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1025e9c-a186-5c7a-9a6b-d73bfd767c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-339475" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm \\text{i}.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-A\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\\,⇒\\,B=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ where $A$ is arbitrary."}]}
